<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    The launcher's own dialect. Everything here is either an x:Key'd ControlTheme or a class
    selector (.tm-*), so it is opt-in and cannot leak into the shared message box, which stays on
    the app-wide control themes by design.

    There is deliberately NOT a single colour literal in this file — every brush is a resource key,
    and Fluent2TokenTests.LauncherStyles_ContainNoColourLiterals fails the build if a hex value
    appears anywhere, PROSE INCLUDED. Any Aero colour quoted in a comment below is therefore a
    decimal RGB triple.

    TWO LAYERS SUPPLY THOSE KEYS SINCE ROUND 7, TASK 7, and which one a rule names is not a style
    choice. Themes/Fluent2/Tokens.axaml owns the app's semantic names — surfaces, ink, hairlines,
    the shape scale, the spacing ladder and the type sizes — and since Task 7 its values are Aero's.
    Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Brushes.axaml owns the STATEFUL fills: Aero paints a button, a list row and a
    text field as two- and four-stop vertical gradients that differ per state, and a token layer of
    flat SolidColorBrushes has no gradient to give. So a rest surface, an ink colour or a hairline
    names a token; a hover, pressed or selected FILL names the Aero brush directly. Fluent2TokenTests
    unions the vendored files into its defined set for exactly that reason, and it does NOT filter on
    the "Aero" prefix, so a typo in one of those names still fails the build.

    All brush references use {DynamicResource}, not {StaticResource}: the app switches
    ThemeVariant live when the OS theme or the appearance setting changes, and a StaticResource
    would freeze the launcher in whatever variant happened to be active at parse time.

    ── The focus visual ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Every FocusAdorner below is the CLASSIC DOTTED RECTANGLE: a Rectangle stroked in
    FocusStrokeOuterBrush (= AeroForeground) at thickness 1 with StrokeDashArray="1,1". Windows 11's
    two-ring rectangle — a 2 px outer ring in the ink colour plus a 1 px inner ring in the opposite
    one — went with FocusStrokeInnerBrush, the token that made the second ring possible. Task 3
    verified with a synthetic VK_TAB that Avalonia's default adorner does draw on the vendored Aero
    Button (SetFocus() alone shows nothing: Control.OnGotFocus builds an adorner only for
    NavigationMethod.Tab or Directional), so what an Aero button showed on Tab was still Fluent's
    ring; these five adorners are what replace it on everything this file themes.

    THE MARGIN IS 2 AND IT MUST NEVER GO NEGATIVE. AN ADORNER IS CLIPPED. Task 7's first cut wrote
    Margin="-2" on all five, meaning "draw just outside the control", and it rendered NOTHING —
    measured on the shipped build, Light, PrintWindow flag 2 at 150 %: a synthetic VK_TAB moved
    keyboard focus onto the rail's 退出 row (a TmNavRowButton; UIA reported HasKeyboardFocus and a
    60x54 px rect — that row was 40x36 DIP on the icon-only rail; Round 7, Task 8 widened the pane and
    the same row is 156 DIP wide now, at a HEIGHT THAT FOLLOWS THE CONFIGURED FONT AND THE UI LANGUAGE —
    MEASURED 22.67 to 24.67 DIP across the four configurations tabulated in MainWindow.axaml's
    NavRailPane block, and the 22 the design predicted is reached in none of them; see TmNavRowItem
    below) and the whole-window capture came back byte-identical to the
    resting one, ZERO
    differing pixels. The same probe with the stroke swapped for a plain red FILL at Margin="-8"
    showed why: instead of the 84x78 px block that margin asks for, it painted 48x42 px anchored at
    the row's own top-left corner. Decompiled (ilspycmd, Avalonia 12.1.0) that is
    AdornerLayer.ArrangeOverride, which arranges every adorner into new Rect(adorned.Bounds.Size),
    assigns it AdornerHelper.CalculateAdornerClip(adorned), and lets the composition renderer push a
    second clip of its own because AdornerLayer.IsClipEnabled defaults to true. A negative margin
    slides the stroke out of that pair of clips; a positive one cannot. 2 draws the rectangle 2 DIP
    INSIDE the control's box — which is also where Windows drew it, just inside a button's border
    rather than on top of it — and this fix round measured it drawing on the same 退出 row.
  -->

  <Styles.Resources>

    <!-- ══ Launcher item tile ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The most-repeated control in the app, and a real STANDARD BUTTON rather than a borderless
         icon+text row: a fill, a hairline border and the shape scale's control radius, all visible
         AT REST rather than appearing only on hover. TmButton below gives every other button in the
         app (the settings-card actions) that same look; this theme duplicates its template rather
         than deriving from it via BasedOn so a tile's own quirks — Stretch content alignment for the
         icon+label DockPanel, its own fixed Padding, and disabling by making the WHOLE card recede to
         nothing rather than just dimming it — stay self-contained. Pressing does NOT offset the
         content; the fill changes and the geometry does not.

         ROUND 7, TASK 7 REPLACED THE PAINT AND KEPT THE ANATOMY. The fill was SurfaceLayerBrush —
         white at alpha 179 over whatever was behind it — and the states were a second, translucent
         "state layer" Border washing over it, because those Subtle* alphas were calibrated to sit on
         a transparent rest state and could not stack visibly over a fill that was already there.
         Aero has no alpha washes: it paints a button's four states as four opaque vertical gradients
         (AeroButtonIdleBackground / Hover / Pressed / Disabled, Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Brushes.axaml,
         each built from paired …Color0..Color3 keys). So the state rules now swap Root's OWN
         Background and BorderBrush, and the StateLayer Border — along with the four
         "/template/ Border#StateLayer" rules that were its only readers — is DELETED from this theme
         and from TmButton. Nothing is left that a wash could stack on.

         There is deliberately NO Width Setter here any more. Round 3, Task 4 removed the 小/中/大
         button-size setting and left every tile at one fixed 168 DIP; Round 4, item 3 deleted that
         fixed width too, because it was what forced long labels through TextTrimming. A tile is now
         as wide as its own content: 2 DIP of border + 16 DIP of Padding (both below) + the 20 DIP
         icon box (Image.tm-tile-icon further down) + the icon's own 10 DIP right margin
         (Views/AppPage.axaml) + the label's single-line advance = 48 DIP of chrome plus the label.

         What keeps that from reading as a ragged right edge is the ABSENCE of a HorizontalAlignment
         on the tile Button, so do not add one: each tile stretches to the width of the column
         StackPanel it sits in, and a vertical StackPanel is as wide as its widest child, so a column
         comes out uniform and only differs from the next column. Measured on the running build with
         UI Automation bounding rectangles (Round 4, item 3, on a 74-item folder): exactly one width
         and one right edge per column, and every one of the nine columns a different width, from
         118.67 to 204 DIP.

         A MinWidth and a MaxWidth are equally deliberately absent: MaxWidth would bring the ellipsis
         straight back, and a MinWidth floor would only re-pad the columns that came out short. The
         bound that matters is applied where the screen is actually visible — WindowPlacement clamps
         the fitted window to the work area and AppPage's Body ScrollViewer scrolls whatever is past
         it. Both were exercised at 每列个数 = 2, where the page asked for 5644 DIP, the window was
         clamped to the 5120 DIP work area, and Body reported itself horizontally scrollable. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmTileButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleBackground}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleOuterBorderBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,7" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />

      <!-- An adorner rather than a border: it is painted by the adorner layer and costs no layout
           at all, so showing focus cannot reflow the grid the way a border thickness change would.
           That is the whole reason, and it holds at any margin — the ADORNER is what buys it, not
           the direction the rectangle is offset in. The classic dotted rectangle, inset 2 DIP; see
           this file's header for what it replaced and for the measurement that says why the margin
           is positive rather than the negative one this task first shipped. -->
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                     StrokeThickness="1" StrokeDashArray="1,1" Margin="2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>

      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>

      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- A tile whose shortcut target no longer exists. It stays in place and stays readable —
           it is information, not noise — but it takes no pointer states and cannot be launched, and
           the card recedes to nothing (fill AND border both go transparent) rather than merely
           dimming, so it reads as "not a button" instead of "a greyed-out button". -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      </Style>
      <!-- The disabled tile's ICON is dimmed by a rule in the Styles body below, not here: a
           ControlTheme may not contain a descendant selector, and the icon lives in the launcher's
           DataTemplate rather than in this control template. -->
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Header icon button (☰ nav toggle, ✕ close) ═══════════════════════════════════════════
         A 32×32 square target with no rest-state chrome of its own: transparent until it is hovered
         or pressed, when it takes a LIST ROW's fill rather than the raised command-button gradient
         TmTileButton and TmButton take. Round 7, Task 7 chose that pairing deliberately and it is
         no longer "the same state model as the tile" — the tile's four opaque AeroButton* gradients
         and this theme's two AeroListBoxItem* ones are different vocabularies, because an icon
         button is a toolbar affordance and Aero draws a toolbar affordance as a soft capsule, not
         as a button that pops out of the surface. (Round 3, Task 2 removed this theme's third user,
         the title strip's own ⋯ overflow button — MainWindow.axaml's navigation-pane comment explains
         where its functions went. The one consumer left is Controls/InfoBar.axaml's CloseButton.) -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmIconButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                     StrokeThickness="1" StrokeDashArray="1,1" Margin="2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <!-- THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO Opacity SETTER ON THIS THEME, and the rest state is still a
           glyph at 60 % of the ink. The 60 % lives on TextBlock.tm-icon (in the Styles body below),
           which every consumer's content already carries — Controls/InfoBar.axaml's CloseButton,
           the only one there is, holds a TextBlock with Classes="tm-icon small". Task 7 first put a
           second Opacity 0.6 here as well, on the reasoning that §3.2 #8 replaced the deleted
           TextSecondaryBrush with an opacity; Visual.Opacity NESTS MULTIPLICATIVELY, so that glyph
           rendered at 0.6 x 0.6 = 36 % of TextPrimaryBrush, not 60 %. Over the 应用 pane that
           computes to 2.52:1 in Light and 2.60:1 in Dark — under WCAG's 3:1 non-text floor, in the
           task whose whole point was legibility. Dropping the setter puts the glyph back at the
           0.6 the deleted TextSecondaryBrush measured (alpha 0.605 in Light; Round 7, Task 8 moved
           that measurement out of this file into
           docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md with the rest of the
           icon rail's Task Manager survey).

           NOR IS THERE A :pointerover Opacity RULE, and there was no ink change on hover to lose.
           A Style setter on the glyph outranks anything this Button contributes to it, so the
           pre-Task-7 pair — Foreground TextSecondaryBrush here, TextPrimaryBrush on hover — never
           reached a tm-icon TextBlock either: it sets its own Foreground. Hover's signal is the
           FILL below, and it always was.

           None of this is photographed. InfoBar's only host opens it on
           "state.Status != TaskbarPinStatus.Pinned" (Views/AppPage.axaml.cs) and this machine's
           TanMenu is pinned, which the round's rules forbid changing; the figures above are
           computed from the two setters, not measured. -->
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemHoverBackground}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemSelectedBackground}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Standard button (settings-card actions) ════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The command-button surface, used everywhere a settings card needs an actual button
         (打开/更改…/重置为默认/清理缓存/备份…/恢复…/固定到任务栏/清除/检查更新/下载更新, …). 32 px tall at
         100% scaling — MinHeight rather than a fixed Height, so the actual on-screen height still
         tracks the OS text-scale setting the way every other control here does, instead of being a
         hardcoded pixel box — with the same fill/border/radius shape TmTileButton uses above. See
         that theme's comment for what Round 7 Task 7 changed about the state model, and why the two
         themes duplicate the template instead of one deriving from the other via BasedOn.

         WHY THIS THEME STILL EXISTS at all, now that Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml publishes a
         real {x:Type Button} ControlTheme: the two are not interchangeable. The vendored theme is
         Aero's command button complete with its double border and its own metrics, and the message
         box already renders on it (Dialogs/MessageBoxWindow.axaml carries that measurement). This
         one is the LAUNCHER's button — one MinHeight, one Padding and a Hand cursor, all of which a
         settings row's action strip depends on — and it names Aero's own four background gradients
         for its four states, so the vocabulary is shared even though the template is not.

         Round 7, Task 3 made this the ONLY settings-card button theme. TmAccentButton — a BasedOn
         derivative that swapped the rest-state fill and the text colour to the accent pair — was
         deleted with the Aero control themes, because Windows 7 had no accent-filled default button
         to imitate: the Aero command button is the gradient one and there is exactly one of it. Its
         three call sites (AppPage's PinNowButton, UpdatesCard's UpdateActionBtn, and the run-time
         swap in BehaviorCard.axaml.cs's ApplyTaskbarPinState) all landed here, and each one's own
         comment records what it gave up. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleBackground}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleOuterBorderBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                     StrokeThickness="1" StrokeDashArray="1,1" Margin="2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Aero draws a disabled command button as its OWN pair of gradients rather than as a dimmed
           enabled one, so the disabled state is a real repaint here too. The Opacity 0.6 this rule
           used to carry is gone with it: dimming a gradient that is already the disabled gradient
           would double the signal. -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Nav pane command row ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Round 3, Task 2: 打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 moved out of the title strip's ⋯ overflow flyout
         into their own rows in the navigation pane's bottom-pinned footer (MainWindow.axaml). Three of
         those four are this theme's consumers today — Round 5, item 2 took 关于 out of the pane and made
         it a settings card, and its row went with it.

         THE BUTTON-VERSUS-ListBoxItem SPLIT GOT MORE LOAD-BEARING IN ROUND 7, TASK 8, NOT LESS. The
         page rows below are now the vendored Aero ListBoxItem theme, which brings FOUR states with it,
         one of them :selected. A command row rendered as a ListBoxItem would therefore gain a selected
         gradient it must never show. "A Button has no :selected pseudoclass at all" is still what makes
         that impossible by construction rather than by a style rule a later edit could defeat.

         The SHAPE is Aero's list row, rebuilt here rather than inherited, because a Button cannot be
         BasedOn a ListBoxItem's theme: a Panel holding an OuterBorder that wraps an InnerBorder, with
         the ContentPresenter over the pair, and the OuterBorder hidden outright at rest so an
         un-hovered row paints nothing at all. Structure, brush names and both corner radii are the
         vendored Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ListBoxItem.axaml's own — outer 2 is its CornerRadius setter
         and inner 1 is what its AdjustCornerRadiusConverter Amount="-1" computes from that (VENDORED).
         Both are restated as literals here because this template is ours and cannot template-bind into
         theirs; they are the one pair of numbers in this theme that is not authored.

         Padding "8,3" and Margin "2,1" are IDENTICAL to TmNavRowItem below, and must be: a command row
         has to measure exactly like a page row for the pane to read as one list. Both are AUTHORED as
         part of the pane's 160 DIP derivation, which MainWindow.axaml's NavRailPane carries in full.

         TWO STATES, not Aero's four. A command row can never be :selected, and none of the three is
         ever disabled (each fires immediately). :pressed borrows the SELECTED gradients rather than
         inventing a third pair, which is also what the vendored file does — its
         "^:selected:not(:pointerover),^:pressed" rule names one pair for both. :focus is deliberately
         NOT in the resting rule's exclusion list even though the vendored row puts it there: a command
         row keeps focus after a click, so honouring :focus would leave a fired row wearing a frame that
         reads exactly like the selection this theme exists to make impossible. The dotted FocusAdorner
         is this row's whole focus signal. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmNavRowButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemHoverBackground}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemHoverOuterBorderBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="2" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,3" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="2,1" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                     StrokeThickness="1" StrokeDashArray="1,1" Margin="2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <!-- Transparent rather than unset: a null Background is not hit-testable, so the strip of
               row between the borders and the glyph would swallow the pointer. The vendored row states
               the same fact as a nearly-transparent alpha; this file may not write a colour literal at
               all (Fluent2TokenTests.LauncherStyles_ContainNoColourLiterals greps the prose too), and
               Transparent IS hit-testable in Avalonia, so it is the same guarantee spelled without a
               number. -->
          <Panel Background="Transparent">
            <Border x:Name="OuterBorder"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                    CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
              <Border x:Name="InnerBorder"
                      Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                      BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemHoverInnerBorderBrush}"
                      BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                      CornerRadius="1" />
            </Border>
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
          </Panel>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:not(:pointerover, :pressed) /template/ Border#OuterBorder">
        <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#OuterBorder">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemSelectedOuterBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#InnerBorder">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemSelectedBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemSelectedInnerBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Nav pane page row ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The other row kind: a DESTINATION the window navigates to and stays on (应用, 设置), so unlike
         TmNavRowButton above it is a ListBoxItem and it has a :selected state. Since Round 7, Task 8
         it is the VENDORED Aero ListBoxItem theme with four setters on top, and no template of its own.

         WHAT THE BasedOn BRINGS, in one line: :pointerover, :selected:not(:pointerover),
         :selected:pointerover and :disabled — four states where this theme declared three, and not one
         of them uses the accent. Selection is Aero's own two nested borders over a two-stop gradient
         (VENDORED, Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ListBoxItem.axaml, which is also where the resting row's
         "hide the OuterBorder outright" rule lives). :selected:pointerover is genuinely new here and
         cannot be captured on this machine, which has no pointer; it is inherited, not authored, so
         there is nothing this file could have got wrong about it.

         THE 3x16 DIP ACCENT PILL IS DELETED, and so are the 55 lines of Task Manager measurement that
         derived it — the row box, the pitch, the pill's cap radius, the glyph-displacement finding, the
         alpha solve behind its fills. Every one of them was a measurement of Windows 11's OWN icon rail,
         taken to make this app's 48 DIP icon rail match it. Under a labelled Windows 7 pane they
         describe nothing: there is no 48 DIP pane, no 40x36 DIP row box, no 40 DIP pitch and no accent
         anywhere in the pane. They are archived, with their instrument and the commit at which their
         subject stopped existing, in
         docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md.

         THE FOUR SETTERS, and nothing else:
           · HorizontalContentAlignment Left — a labelled row is read from its leading edge. The
             vendored theme states no alignment at all, so this adds rather than overrides.
           · Padding "8,3" — AUTHORED. The 3 is the row box's vertical inset and the 8 is its leading
             text inset. The row box it produces is NOT the 22 DIP the design predicted, and it is not
             ONE number: the tallest child is the label's line box, not the 16 DIP glyph, so the row
             follows the configured font and the UI language. MEASURED 24.67 DIP at the app's DEFAULT
             font in Chinese, 24.00 at the default font in English, 23.33 with 字体 = Verdana in
             Chinese, 22.67 with Verdana in English. See MainWindow.axaml's NavRailPane block for the
             four measurements and for why the 22 is not chased.
           · Margin "2,1" — AUTHORED. The 1 is what turns the row box into the row pitch, so the pitch
             tracks the row box and is no more constant than it is: MEASURED 26.67 DIP at the app's
             DEFAULT font in either language, 25.33 with 字体 = Verdana in either. The 2 leaves the item
             column 156 DIP inside the 160 DIP pane, which is the span Border.tm-nav-separator further
             down is derived from — and unlike the vertical numbers, that one IS constant, because it is
             a margin rather than a line box; 156 DIP came back in all four configurations.
           · FocusAdorner — the dotted rectangle this file gives all five of its focusable themes. The
             vendored theme declares none, so WITHOUT this setter a Tab onto 应用 would draw Fluent's
             two-ring rectangle over an Aero row. It is not decoration: it is the only thing that keeps
             the header block at the top of this file true.
         Every number above is AUTHORED. There is no Windows 7 machine here and no Aero reference
         capture in this tree; MainWindow.axaml's NavRailPane carries the whole derivation, including
         the published statements it does rest on.

         Padding and Margin are the SAME literals TmNavRowButton states above, and they have to be: a
         command row and a page row must measure alike for the pane to read as one list. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmNavRowItem" TargetType="ListBoxItem"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type ListBoxItem}}">
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,3" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="2,1" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                     StrokeThickness="1" StrokeDashArray="1,1" Margin="2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Styles.Resources>

  <!-- ══ Search header ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 7, Task 1 restored this rule along with the field it frames. It was deleted by commit
       6d00060, which moved search into the window's title strip; the Aero conversion deletes that strip
       (win-window-frames: "Don't add controls to a window frame. Put the controls within the window
       instead.") and the header comes back to Views/AppPage.axaml with it.

       Round 7, Task 7 re-derived the two values the revert carried over. The divider stays a 1 DIP
       hairline and is StrokeDividerBrush, which is now the Aero group-box outline — a divider and a
       group-box edge are one hairline in a Windows 7 dialog. The padding was 12,10 (Fluent's ramp)
       and is 11,7: PUBLISHED, vis-layout's "Dialog box margins — 7 DLU / 11 relative pixels" for the
       horizontal component and "Between related controls — 4 / 7" for the vertical. The field's own
       Aero treatment is still Task 9's. -->

  <Style Selector="Border.tm-header">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeDividerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,7" />
  </Style>


  <!-- Iconography is Segoe Fluent Icons, and it is the ONE deliberate Windows 11 anachronism this
       conversion keeps: Windows 7 had no icon font at all — its shell glyphs were bitmap resources —
       so there is nothing to convert to. Segoe MDL2 Assets stands in on Windows 10 at the same code
       points. Both are system fonts; nothing is bundled.

       The rest contrast is 60 % of the ink, not a grey of its own. TextSecondaryBrush and
       TextTertiaryBrush carried it until Round 7, Task 7 deleted both: Windows 7 published no
       secondary body colour, and inventing a third grey beside AeroForeground and
       AeroDisabledForeground is worse than one opacity that follows the variant for free (the
       design's §3.2 #8, applied here as well as to a card's subtitle). -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon">
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource IconFontFamily}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.6" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Quieter icons: the search affordance and the folder hint are labels, not actions. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon.subtle">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon.small">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The search field is a plain TextBox stripped to text only; the field's own shape, fill and
       outline are drawn by the Border it sits in (Border.tm-searchfield). Background and
       BorderThickness are set as LOCAL values on the element in Views/AppPage.axaml, and a local
       value outranks a style setter for the same property on the same element.

       WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, corrected in Round 7 Task 4: it settles the OUTER TextBox's
       own Background and BorderThickness and nothing else. A theme's state rules reach a NAMED PART
       inside the control template, which carries no local value of its own, so they are not blocked
       by anything set out here. Under Fluent that part was Border#PART_BorderElement; under the
       vendored Aero theme (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml) it is Border#BgElement, whose
       Background is a TemplateBinding onto the outer Background and therefore DOES start out as the
       local Transparent — at rest. The two rules that undo it are near the tm-search style below,
       beside the write-up of what Aero actually repaints and what that measured. -->

  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxIdleBorderBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxIdleBackground}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7,0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Round 7, Task 7: this Border draws the field, so its three states now name Aero's own text-box
       brushes rather than the deleted SurfaceLayer*/StrokeControl/TextTertiary tokens. Aero's idle
       border is a two-stop gradient and its hover border is the blue one — that IS Aero's hover
       signal, and it is the same brush the vendored TextBox template would have drawn if this field
       were painting itself (it is not: Views/AppPage.axaml gives the inner TextBox a local
       Background="Transparent" and BorderThickness="0", and the two /template/ rules further down
       are what stop Aero repainting the part underneath). -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxHoverBorderBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The focus signal is a fill change plus the accent underline below, and NOT a full ring around
       the field: the resting border stays on all four sides (this rule leaves BorderBrush and
       BorderThickness alone). The 2 px underline on the bottom edge only is a separate element —
       Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar, inside SearchField in Views/AppPage.axaml — because a Border
       cannot draw a differently-coloured thickness on one side while keeping the other three at the
       ambient resting colour: BorderBrush is a single brush for every side. Round 3 Task 1 reached
       that shape against Windows 11; Round 7 Task 7 kept it and swapped the fill to Aero's own focus
       background. Task 9 owns whether the accent underline survives the conversion at all. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield.focused">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxFocusBackground}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The bar itself: transparent at rest, so it takes no visual space and needs no separate
       Opacity/IsVisible toggle. IsHitTestVisible="False" (set in Views/AppPage.axaml, not here — a
       ControlTheme/Style cannot express it any earlier than the element's own local markup) keeps it
       from ever intercepting a click meant for the TextBox underneath. CornerRadius "0,0,2,2" is
       RadiusControlInner (RadiusControl minus one) on the bottom corners only, and Margin "1,0,1,1"
       insets it a hairline inside SearchField's own 1 px border and radius — the same "sit just inside
       the parent's border" idea RadiusControlInner already documents in Tokens.axaml, reused here
       instead of a second literal. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield.focused Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentDefaultBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- There is deliberately NO :disabled rule for this field. Round 6, item 1 established that on a
       page that cannot be searched the field is not shown at all rather than greyed out; Round 7,
       Task 1 made that structural — the field is inside the 应用 page now, so a page that is not 应用
       simply does not contain it and MainWindow's old "SearchField.IsVisible = …" write is deleted.
       Either way nothing in this app puts this field into :disabled and a rule for that state would
       have no producer left. -->

  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-search">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CaretBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <!-- There is deliberately no ":disabled" companion to the rule above either. It used to repaint
       Foreground and PlaceholderForeground to TextDisabledBrush, because the Foreground set above at
       Style precedence outranks a TextBox ControlTheme's own built-in :disabled foreground and so
       left the text at full contrast. A page that cannot be searched does not contain this field at
       all any more (Round 6 item 1's decision, made structural by Round 7 Task 1), so nothing in
       this app puts this TextBox into :disabled and the theme's own :disabled rules — Aero's, since
       Round 7 Task 4 — have no producer to fire on. -->

  <!-- ══ Suppressing the theme's own fill, at the two states that repaint it ══════════════════
       This field's fill is Border.tm-searchfield's, above. The TextBox inside it is meant to
       contribute nothing but glyphs, which is why Views/AppPage.axaml gives it a LOCAL
       Background="Transparent" and BorderThickness="0".

       That local pair is enough at rest and only at rest. Aero's TextBox template
       (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml) binds Border#BgElement's Background to the outer
       Background — so at rest the part inherits the local Transparent — and then declares
       :pointerover and :focus rules that assign that same part an OPAQUE fill directly. A local
       value on the outer control cannot reach a named part inside the template, and a style setter
       outranks the TemplateBinding that carried the local value there, so both states repaint.

       Round 3 Task 1 wrote the :focus one of these two against Fluent's part name,
       Border#PART_BorderElement, to kill an accent band. Round 7 Task 4 replaced Fluent's TextBox
       template wholesale, that part name stopped existing, and it had to be re-aimed at what Aero
       actually does — and gained a :pointerover twin, because Aero repaints on hover too. The design
       document expected to DELETE them outright on the grounds that Aero signals focus with a
       BorderBrush change rather than a band. Two things it did not account for, both checked here:
       Aero's :focus and :pointerover rules change the BACKGROUND as well as the border brush, and
       the border-brush half of the signal cannot draw at all on this field, because neither Aero
       rule sets BorderThickness and the local 0 therefore still holds. Deleting them would leave
       only the background half — an opaque rectangle covering the TextBox's own rect but not the
       icon or Border.tm-searchfield's 10 DIP padding, i.e. a lighter block inset inside the field.

       Only Background is put back, and only at the two states that take it: BorderBrush is left to
       Aero, since at BorderThickness 0 it paints nothing, and adding a setter for it would be a rule
       with no pixels behind it. Border.tm-searchfield.focused stays the ONLY focus signal that
       reaches the screen, which is the conclusion Round 3 Task 1 reached and this keeps.

       MEASURED, this build against one with these two rules deleted. PrintWindow flag 2, 150 %
       scale, the field focused (the app focuses it on summon, so this is its everyday state). Three
       samples on one row through the middle of the field: one in its own 10 DIP left padding, two
       inside the TextBox's rect.

         DARK, rules deleted : padding (49,49,49)   interior (93,93,93) (93,93,93)
         DARK, rules present : padding (49,49,49)   interior (49,49,49) (49,49,49)
         LIGHT, either way   : padding (255,255,255) interior (255,255,255) (255,255,255)

       (93,93,93) is AeroTextBoxFocusBackground's Dark value exactly — an opaque block covering the
       TextBox's rect and stopping at the padding, which is what "a lighter rectangle inset inside
       the field" looks like when you sample it. LIGHT HIDES IT COMPLETELY: that token's Light value
       is white and the field already renders white, so a Light-only check would have passed a build
       that is visibly wrong in Dark. The accent underline reads (0,103,192) Light / (76,194,255)
       Dark across 3 physical rows = 2 DIP, unchanged by any of this. -->

  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-search:pointerover /template/ Border#BgElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-search:focus /template/ Border#BgElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Tile icon size ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 3, Task 4 removed the 小/中/大 button-size setting, which used to drive tile width, icon
       box and type size together as three size classes (Panel.size-small/medium/large). This rule is
       what is left of that: one icon box, with no size class left to scope it under.

       Round 4, item 3 then deleted TmTileButton's own Width Setter, which makes this 20x20 more
       load-bearing than it looks — it is no longer just how big the icon draws, it is a term in every
       tile's WIDTH (48 DIP of chrome = 2 border + 16 padding + 20 here + 10 icon margin). TmTileButton
       above still carries the FontSize, the other term the label's advance depends on. Note that this
       is a class-scoped style, i.e. a FrameType.Style frame that a logical-tree detach drops, whereas
       TmTileButton is an element-local theme frame that survives one — see MainWindow.RefitToContent's
       guard, which item 3 made more load-bearing for exactly this reason. -->
  <Style Selector="Image.tm-tile-icon">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="20" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A tile whose target is missing: the label greys out via the ControlTheme, and the icon fades
       with it so the whole tile reads as unavailable rather than half-lit. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.tm-tile:disabled Image.tm-tile-icon">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Sections ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- The 应用 page's category heading. It was Body Strong on the Windows 11 ramp — 14/20 SemiBold in
       TextSecondaryBrush. Round 7, Task 7 gives it the SAME treatment as the 设置 page's group
       headings (TextBlock.tm-group-head at the bottom of this file): FontSizeGroupHead and
       HeadingBrush, both PUBLISHED by
       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-fonts, which gives group headers as
       11 pt in a blue this file may not quote as hex — Tokens.axaml's HeadingBrush carries the value
       and the citation. A labelled group is a labelled group whichever page it is on, and Aero got
       its heading hierarchy from colour rather than from size or weight; the SemiBold is kept anyway
       because 11 pt is only 3 DIP above body here. The FACE stays inherited rather than pinned: a
       user who picked a font in settings expects it here too. Task 9 owns the 应用 page's final
       geometry and may move this. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-section">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeGroupHead}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource HeadingBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A group backed by a real folder is clickable; the built-in 常用工具 group has no Directory and
       stays inert, which is why the affordance is a class the view sets rather than a blanket rule.
       Padding 4,3 became 5,5 in Round 7 Task 7: 5 is vis-layout's published minimum separation
       ("at least 3 DLUs (5 relative pixels)"), and 3 was on no ladder at all. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="5,5" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxItemHoverBackground}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable:pointerover TextBlock.tm-folder">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-folder">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Empty / no-results states ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="22,22" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <!-- 60 % of the ink rather than a grey of its own — the mechanism that replaced
       TextSecondaryBrush; TextBlock.tm-icon above carries the reasoning. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty > TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.6" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Avalonia's ContentControl defaults HorizontalContentAlignment to Left, not Center as WPF
       does, so an ordinary Button renders its label against the left edge. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty Button">
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The page's one big heading, at the same FontSizeSubtitle the 设置 page's title uses. That token
       is 16 now, not 20: vis-fonts gives a main instruction as 12 pt = 16 DIP, and the vendored
       AeroFontSizeLarge agrees exactly. Full ink and full opacity, against the 60 % body lines above.
       The FACE comes from the view model (TitleFontFamily) only while the user has not chosen a font
       of their own; pinning it here would silently override that setting. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty > TextBlock.title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,5" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Update notice ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A whole-surface Button, so it is Aero's command button and this rule states only what the
       banner adds to it: the stretch, the padding and the body size. Background, BorderBrush,
       BorderThickness and CornerRadius are deliberately ABSENT — the element carries no Theme=, so
       Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml's {x:Type Button} ControlTheme already paints all four states,
       and setting them here would freeze one gradient across every state. They were
       SubtleFillSecondaryBrush over StrokeControlBrush at RadiusControl until Round 7, Task 7
       deleted the first of those tokens. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.tm-update">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,7" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Taskbar-pin InfoBar ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Controls/InfoBar's surface, and a Border rather than a Button — this bar's actions are its own
       child buttons, not the whole surface, so it cannot borrow the command button's gradients the
       way the update notice above does.

       Re-derived to the GROUP-BOX vocabulary in Round 7, Task 7, which is what Windows 7 drew around
       a labelled region: a hairline outline at the control radius, no fill of its own. It was a
       SubtleFillSecondaryBrush wash inside a StrokeControlBrush hairline; the wash token is gone and
       an unfilled outline is the Aero answer rather than a substitute for one. StrokeControlBrush IS
       the group-box outline now (Tokens.axaml aliases it onto AeroGroupBoxBorderBrush), so the
       hairline needs no new key. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-infobar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,7" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Toast ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A transient, self-clearing error. Sits in the overlay layer and contributes no layout size,
       so it can never resize the popup.

       Round 7, Task 7: the outline moved from StrokeSurfaceFlyoutBrush to StrokeControlBrush (both
       tokens resolved to different values; the first is deleted, and the group-box hairline is the
       one outline Aero has) and the BoxShadow is DELETED with ShadowFlyout. Aero drew no soft shadow
       inside a client area — elevation was the window frame's job, and this element is not a window.
       The surface stays SurfaceBaseBrush, which is now AeroWindowBackground: a transient panel over
       the page reads as a small piece of window. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-toast">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBaseBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,7" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="11" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Bottom" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-toast > TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The pane's ink ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       tm-icon (further up) draws every glyph in the app at 60 % of the ink; the navigation pane draws
       its own at full ink, and since Round 7, Task 8 that is a statement about a pane whose rows carry
       VISIBLE LABELS rather than about an icon rail.

       The rule is KEPT and RE-DERIVED. Its Round 6 justification was a lookup — Task Manager's rail
       glyphs solved to alpha 0.893 black in Light and pure white in Dark, which is this app's
       TextPrimaryBrush to the level — and that reference is void twice over: this app is not matching
       Windows 11 any more, and the thing being matched was an icon-only rail. What replaces it is
       Aero's own structure rather than a number: a labelled row's glyph and its label are ONE piece of
       ink. tm-nav-label below states no Opacity, so it inherits the full AeroForeground; a glyph left
       at tm-icon's 0.6 beside it would read as a disabled icon next to an enabled word. Both at 1 is
       the only pair that says "one row, one label".

       Scoped to the pane, and nothing else, through NavRailContent's own Classes="tm-nav-rail"
       (MainWindow.axaml) — the class that file kept for exactly this. The descendant combinator reaches
       all five rows on the logical tree, ListBox to ListBoxItem to DockPanel to TextBlock and Button to
       DockPanel to TextBlock alike. tm-icon's own Opacity further up is untouched, so the search
       affordance, the folder hint and the settings cards' icons keep theirs. -->
  <Style Selector="Grid.tm-nav-rail TextBlock.tm-icon">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The pane row's label ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 7, Task 8 gave the navigation pane visible labels, so this class exists again. It is not
       a revert of the class Round 4, item 1 deleted: that one had to be hidden and shown as the rail
       animated between two widths, and there is still exactly ONE width.

       The 6 DIP leading gap is the icon-to-label gap in the pane's own 160 DIP derivation
       (Margin 2 + Padding 8 + icon 16 + gap 6 = 32 DIP before the label; MainWindow.axaml's
       NavRailPane carries the whole sum). It lives here rather than on the five rows so that the five
       row markups state a glyph and a name, and this file stays the one place the number is written.
       AUTHORED, like every other number in that derivation.

       No Foreground and no Opacity: a label takes the window's own Foreground, which MainWindow.axaml
       sets to TextPrimaryBrush, at full strength — see the pane-ink rule directly above for why the
       glyph beside it is lifted to match rather than the other way round. NoWrap + CharacterEllipsis
       because the label column is a fixed 118 DIP and the strings are user-selectable language data:
       a longer translation must trim on ONE LINE, never reflow into two and push every row below it
       down. No pitch figure is quoted here on purpose — the pitch follows the configured font (see
       TmNavRowItem above), and this rule holds whatever it currently measures. The row's
       AutomationProperties.Name (ApplyRailText, MainWindow.axaml.cs) carries the untrimmed text for a
       screen reader either way. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-nav-label">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="6,0,0,0" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="NoWrap" />
    <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The footer's one group boundary ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A single hairline separating the terminal action from the reversible rows. Round 4, item 2 put it
       UNDER 退出, which was then the footer's first row; Round 5, item 1 moved 退出 to the bottom at the
       user's request and the hairline travelled with it, so it now sits ABOVE 退出 and below 设置. Its
       position is MainWindow.axaml's business — that file's footer StackPanel carries the ordering
       rationale and the reason the fence has to follow the row.

       BOTH NUMBERS ARE DERIVED, NOT PICKED, AND ROUND 7, TASK 8 MOVED THE HORIZONTAL ONE. It is the
       rows' own Margin — "2,1", set by both TmNavRowItem and TmNavRowButton — so the line spans exactly
       the 156 DIP item column (160 DIP pane − 2×2 DIP item margin) and stops flush with the row boxes
       instead of running edge-to-edge across the pane. It was 4, against a 40 DIP item column in a
       48 DIP pane, and that pane is gone. The VERTICAL 4 is unchanged and still puts 4 DIP of clear
       space on each side of the 1 DIP line; with the neighbouring rows' own 1 DIP vertical margins that
       is 5 DIP between the line and each adjacent row box, against the 2 DIP that separates two
       ordinary rows — enough to read as a boundary, not so much that it reads as a missing row.

       The block is 9 DIP tall (1 + 2×4), and THAT number is constant — it is margins and a hairline.
       THE FOOTER IT SITS IN IS NOT, because four of its terms are row pitches and a row pitch follows
       the configured font and the UI language (TmNavRowItem above). MEASURED with UI Automation on the
       shipped build, from NavRail's own bottom edge to the client's: 184 px = 122.67 DIP at the app's
       DEFAULT font in Chinese, 180 px = 120.00 DIP at the default font in English, 176 px = 117.33 DIP
       with 字体 = Verdana in Chinese, and 172 px = 114.67 DIP with Verdana in English. The design
       predicted 109 from "four rows at a 24 DIP pitch + 9 + 4"; run that same sum at the default font's
       real 26.67 pitch in Chinese and it gives 106.67 + 9 + 4 = 119.67 against the measured 122.67,
       with NavFooterRail's own wrapper plus 150 % layout rounding accounting for the remaining 3 DIP —
       measured in that configuration, the ListBox is 41 px tall around a 37 px row. NO configuration
       measured here comes near 109. It was 173 DIP on the 48 DIP icon rail's 40 DIP pitch, and 213
       before Round 5, item 2 took the 关于 row out of the footer.

       StrokeDividerBrush is the right token and always was: a divider is exactly what this is, and being
       a DynamicResource it follows the light/dark swap for free. Round 7, Task 7 re-pointed it onto the
       vendored Aero group-box outline, on the ground that a divider and a group-box edge are one
       hairline in a Windows 7 dialog, so this line now matches the 设置 page's own rules by
       construction. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-nav-separator">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="2,4" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeDividerBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The content pane ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MainWindow.axaml's PagePane — the Border that hosts whichever page is showing. The two rules
       below are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE by construction (.app against :not(.app)), not a base rule plus an
       override, and that is deliberate: see "WHY THESE LIVE HERE" at the end.

       ROUND 7, TASK 7 REWROTE THIS BLOCK RATHER THAN EDITING IT, and the reason is worth stating
       once. What stood here was a 147-line argument about Mica: it measured this window's Mica
       backdrop at 238..247 in Light and 30..34 in Dark, showed that those numbers STRADDLE
       SurfaceBaseBrush rather than reaching SurfacePageBrush, and concluded that a translucent
       settings card would still separate from a transparent 设置 pane. Task 1 falsified its first
       premise — the window asks for AcrylicBlur now and this machine grants it — and its second, by
       moving the search field it cited as "a settings card on Mica is already on screen" onto the
       white 应用 pane. Task 7 falsified what was left: there is no card fill any more (Border.tm-card
       below), so there is no Δ to preserve, and the tokens the whole table was arithmetic over —
       SurfaceLayerBrush, SubtleFillSecondaryBrush, SubtleFillTertiaryBrush — no longer exist. Its
       Windows 11 measurements are archived in
       docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md as the record of a build
       this one replaced.

       ── 应用 (the .app rule) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       An opaque pane with a 1 DIP hairline on its TOP and LEFT edges only, square corners, flush
       against the rail and against the caption with zero inset. It is not a floating card and it
       never was.

       The FILL is SurfacePageBrush, which Task 7 aliased onto the vendored AeroListBoxBackground.
       That is Explorer's own content area: in Windows 7 the item view was a list control and its
       background was the list background, so the 应用/其他 pane distinction survives the conversion
       with an Aero justification instead of the Windows 11 Settings one it was given in Round 4.

       The BORDER is StrokeControlBrush, which Task 7 aliased onto AeroGroupBoxBorderBrush — the one
       hairline Windows 7 draws around a bounded region. It replaces a black-at-alpha-15 token whose
       whole derivation was "15/255 over white predicts 240", an argument about a translucent stroke
       this layer no longer contains. 1 DIP rather than the 1.33 that 2 physical px at 150 % would
       imply: 2 px IS a 1 DIP stroke layout-rounded at this scale, and 1.33 would be wrong at every
       other one.

       The CORNER is 0, and that is VENDORED rather than authored: the 应用 pane used to carry
       RadiusPaneTopLeft, an 8 DIP arc on the top-left corner alone, measured off a Task Manager
       capture. Aero's own ListBox sets CornerRadius="0" (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ListBox.axaml)
       and a Windows 7 content pane has no rounded corner at all, so the token is deleted and its
       20-line arc measurement is archived with the rest.

       The 1 DIP left and top borders are laid out INSIDE this Border, so they take 1 DIP each away
       from the page. MainWindow.RefitToContent hands that DIP back through PaneBorderDip, and
       WindowHost.ChromeHeightDip does the same for the top one — see both constants' own remarks. Do
       not change this thickness without changing those.

       ── every other page (the :not(.app) rule) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Paints NOTHING: Transparent, no border, no corner, so the window's own surface runs unbroken
       from the rail and the caption straight through the content. That is Round 6 item 2's decision
       (除了应用页，其他页面使用和窗口相同的材质面板) and Task 7 keeps it to the letter — these three
       setters are byte-identical to the ones that shipped.

       WHAT CHANGED IS WHAT SHOWS THROUGH, AND IT CHANGED IN MainWindow.axaml, NOT HERE. The window's
       client area is opaque now: the content Grid below the caption paints SurfaceBaseBrush, i.e.
       AeroWindowBackground. That is Aero's own structure — glass was the FRAME, the client area was
       (252,252,252) — and it is what makes this pane's ink legible, because a transparent pane over the
       acrylic puts every glyph on a live blur of an arbitrary desktop. MEASURED rather than argued:
       on this machine PrintWindow photographs the transparent 设置 pane as (0,0,0), so its own ink
       could not be sampled at all, and the wallpaper the acrylic is a blur of averages (87,84,103)
       over its 7680x4532 pixels — against which the page's group headings compute to 1.4:1 and its
       body ink to 2.9:1. MainWindow.axaml's content Grid carries that arithmetic in full.

       Background is Transparent and is never simply omitted. It states the intent where a missing
       setter reads as an oversight, and a null-background Border is not hit-testable in Avalonia, so
       the gaps between settings cards would stop absorbing pointer input.

       ── WHY THESE LIVE HERE, AND NOT AS LOCAL VALUES ON THE BORDER ─────────────────────────────────
       Because a style setter cannot override a local value in Avalonia, in either direction. A class
       selector's setters are applied at BindingPriority.StyleTrigger, which the framework's own
       documentation on that member describes as overriding a TEMPLATE value; LocalValue is a strictly
       higher priority than both (Avalonia 12.1.0's BindingPriority: Animation, LocalValue, StyleTrigger,
       Template, Style, Inherited, Unset). The app already relies on the same ranking from the other
       side — see the SearchBox TextBox's own remarks in Views/AppPage.axaml, where a LOCAL Background is
       what stops the theme's :pointerover rule repainting the field. So writing the 应用 look as
       Background/BorderThickness/CornerRadius attributes on PagePane and then trying to switch it with a
       class toggle would silently do nothing at all: the pane would stay white everywhere. Both states
       are therefore styles, and they are written as non-overlapping selectors so the result does not
       depend on this file's rule ORDER either. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-page-pane.app">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePageBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1,1,0,0" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-page-pane:not(.app)">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ 应用 categories ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NO MaxWidth on EITHER of the two rules below — the panel Border as much as the StackPanel it
       wraps. A cap would only clamp the width the group REPORTS to the horizontal
       StackPanel of categories; the columns ItemsControl inside (AppPage.axaml, bound to
       MenuGroupVm.Columns) keeps laying out however many columns MenuLayout.ChunkIntoColumns
       produced and nothing clips it, so a category with more columns than the cap allows would
       render straight over its right-hand neighbour. Round 4, item 3 removed the tile's fixed width,
       which changes the premise but not the conclusion: a column is as wide as its own widest label,
       so a group's honest desired width is a finite sum of finite column widths — still bounded, and
       still what the scroller needs. Round 6, item 4 added the Border around the StackPanel, and a cap
       there would fail the same way one level further out.

       VerticalAlignment="Top" stays on the StackPanel and is deliberately ABSENT from the Border, and
       the pair is what makes the panels equal height with their content hugging the top. In a
       horizontal StackPanel a child at the default Stretch is arranged to the row's height, so every
       panel Border comes out as tall as the tallest category; the inner StackPanel's Top then keeps
       each heading and tile grid at the top of its own panel and leaves the slack at the bottom of the
       shorter ones. Ragged panels were the alternative and were rejected: measured today the five
       categories are 231.33 / 431.33 / 431.33 / 271.33 / 271.33 DIP, and five bordered boxes of five
       different heights side by side read as an error, where Windows 11 Settings' own large
       side-by-side tiles are equal height. It costs nothing in the fit, because Stretch changes the
       ARRANGE and the panel's DESIRED height is still its own content — WindowHost.FitToContent
       measures the tallest exactly as before. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-group">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Round 6, item 4 (应用页面每个分类增加一个面板样式): each category on the 应用 page sits in a
       panel. This is a deliberate DEPARTURE from Windows 11 rather than a match to it, and saying so is
       the point of this paragraph: the reference draws no container around a labelled group at all —
       measured on ms-settings:privacy, where the three group labels' ink starts at x = 500/500/501
       against their row cards' outer edge at x = 498, i.e. the label sits on the page above a bare
       stack of cards. What the reference supplied, and what this panel's shape came from, was the
       scale of its large side-by-side containers — a measurement Round 7, Task 7 retired along with
       the RadiusPanel token that carried it; it is archived in
       docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md.

       ROUND 7, TASK 7 REPLACED THE FILL AND THE RADIUS, AND RETIRED THE MEASUREMENT THAT PICKED THEM.
       The fill was SubtleFillTertiaryBrush, chosen by compositing three alpha tokens over the white
       应用 pane and taking the one that landed 6 levels below it and collided only with a tile's
       transient pressed wash; the radius was RadiusPanel, an authored 8 derived from Windows 11
       Settings' large side-by-side tiles. BOTH TOKENS ARE DELETED. What replaces them is not another
       measurement but a different OBJECT: this is an Aero group box now — a hairline outline at the
       control radius with no fill of its own, which is exactly what a Windows 7 property page drew
       around a labelled group. StrokeControlBrush is that hairline (Tokens.axaml aliases it onto
       AeroGroupBoxBorderBrush), so no new key is minted, and the separation the old fill bought is
       bought by the outline instead. The tile inside is no longer a translucent outline either — it
       is an opaque Aero command button, so nothing here has to make it visible.

       Task 9 owns this panel's final form: the design gives the 应用 page its own task, and the
       vendored ControlStyles/HeaderedContentControl.axaml — the real Aero group box, with its label
       notched into the top edge — lands there. This rule is the token-layer half of the change and
       says so rather than pretending to be the finished object.

       PADDING 12,12,8,8 is UNCHANGED and is deliberately NOT re-derived onto the 5/7/11 ladder here.
       It is a UNIFORM 12 DIP visible inset whose asymmetry corrects for the tile grid's own
       Margin="0,0,4,4" (AppPage.axaml) — measured on the running build, where the single-column
       常用工具 group reports 122.67 DIP against its tile's 118.67, and the 工具 group reports
       431.33 = 31.33 + 10 x (36 + 4). Both terms of that correction live on the 应用 page, so moving
       one without the other would break the inset; Task 9 moves the pair.

       NOT Border.tm-card, though the anatomy looks similar. tm-card encodes a specific 设置 row — its
       Border must wrap one Auto,*,Auto Grid holding exactly one tm-card-title and one tm-card-sub, and
       SettingsRowTests enforces that over Views/Settings. A category panel has none of those parts, so
       reusing the class would be a semantic collision that passes CI today only because that test's
       CardsDir does not reach Views/AppPage.axaml — one careless widening away from failing four tests
       at once. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-group-panel">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,12,8,8" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ 设置 rows ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A row on a property page: an icon, a title + secondary line, and the control itself trailing.
       The INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE is Round 5 item 3's and survives the conversion untouched — it is
       the most durable thing in this file, and SettingsRowTests holds the parts of it no style can.

       WHAT ROUND 7, TASK 7 TOOK AWAY IS THE CARD. It was a Windows 11 row card: a translucent
       SurfaceLayerBrush fill inside a hairline at the control radius. Windows 7 drew no such object —
       a property page grouped rows inside a GROUP BOX and drew nothing at all around an individual
       row — so the fill, the outline and the radius all go and the class keeps only its spacing. The
       class NAME stays tm-card, because four SettingsRowTests facts and six card files are written
       against it and renaming it would be churn with no pixels behind it.

       The group box that is supposed to be under these rows is Task 9's: the design vendors
       ControlStyles/HeaderedContentControl.axaml there. Until it lands a row sits directly on the
       window's client surface, which is opaque AeroWindowBackground (see the content-pane block
       above), so the row is legible on its own — that was the gate on this task and it is measured in
       the Round 7 Task 7 report rather than asserted here.

       PADDING 16,12 became 11,7 and MARGIN 0,0,0,4 became 0,0,0,5, all three PUBLISHED by
       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout: 11 relative pixels is the
       dialog margin, 7 is "between related controls", and 5 is the minimum separation two target
       regions may have without touching. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-card">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,7" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,5" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Round 5, item 3 pinned BOTH classes' LineHeight, so a title-over-subtitle header block always
       measured exactly 20 + 16 = 36 DIP and one VerticalAlignment="Center" put the icon and the
       trailing control on the same y on every row. ROUND 7, TASK 7 DELETED ALL THREE LineHeight
       TOKENS and both setters with them: Windows 11's ramp publishes a line height beside every size
       and Aero's does not — GDI text has one natural line box per face and size, and inventing a
       second number for it would be a Fluent habit carried into a theme that has no opinion on it.
       The invariant the pinning protected is unaffected, because it never depended on the block
       being 36: it depends on every header being the SAME two lines, which is
       SettingsRowTests.EveryCardHeader_IsOneTitleLineAndOneSubtitleLine's job and which no card
       violates. What the block measures now is one FontSizeBody line over one FontSizeBody line at
       60 % opacity, both at Segoe UI's own leading, and the Round 7 Task 7 report records the number.

       The Round 5 measurement that justified the pinning — a title line box of 19.33 DIP against the
       token's 20, a subtitle box of 16.67 against 16, 0.67 DIP of drift between an ASCII and a CJK
       subtitle, and the four cards whose height moved by 1-2 physical px when the setters went in —
       described a build with a 14/20 ramp and a 12/16 caption size. This one has neither. Those
       numbers are archived in docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md.

       A SUBTITLE IS THE TITLE'S INK AT 60 %, not a colour of its own. TextSecondaryBrush carried it
       until Task 7 deleted it — Windows 7 published no secondary body colour, and inventing a third
       grey beside AeroForeground and AeroDisabledForeground is worse than one opacity that follows
       the variant for free (§3.2 #8). It is also the SAME size as the title now: FontSizeCaption is
       deleted, because a Windows 7 property sheet uses one body size and gets its hierarchy from
       colour, not from a fourth step on a ramp. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-sub">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.6" />
    <!-- Wrap rather than overflow: the middle column is whatever is left after the leading icon and
         the trailing control, which is narrower than several of these descriptions' natural width. -->
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The row spec's shared classes ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 5, item 3 (设置页每个设置项统一设计规范). One anatomy for every settings row: a 16 DIP icon,
       a header of EXACTLY one title line and one subtitle line, the control trailing in column 2, and
       an optional expansion row starting at column 1's own origin. Everything in that anatomy which
       has an AvaloniaProperty behind it is a class here.

       ONE PART OF THE ANATOMY CANNOT BE A STYLE. Grid exposes ShowGridLinesProperty, RowSpacingProperty,
       ColumnSpacingProperty, ColumnProperty, RowProperty, ColumnSpanProperty, RowSpanProperty and
       IsSharedSizeScopeProperty — and no ColumnDefinitionsProperty or RowDefinitionsProperty at all
       (checked by reflection over the shipped Avalonia.Controls.dll 12.1.0). ColumnDefinitions and
       RowDefinitions are plain CLR properties, and a Setter needs an AvaloniaProperty, so
       ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" is written literally in every card and guarded by a source test
       instead (tests/TanMenu.Core.Tests/Themes/SettingsRowTests.cs).

       RowSpacing IS a StyledProperty and could have carried the header-to-expansion gap. It deliberately
       does not: a row gap is charged between row definitions even when the expansion row's only child is
       IsVisible="False" (UpdatesCard's ProgressBar), which would leave 8 DIP of dead space on that card
       in its normal state. The gap lives on the expansion child's own Margin instead (tm-card-expander),
       and an invisible child contributes no margin at all.

       These rules are declared AFTER the two they refine, the same ordering this file already relies on
       for TextBlock.tm-icon.subtle overriding TextBlock.tm-icon's Opacity. -->

  <!-- The icon box. :is(Control) rather than a TextBlock-scoped selector so the same class boxes a row
       whose leading mark is not a glyph without a second rule — which part C then made real: the 关于
       card's mark is the product tile, an Image (Views/Settings/AboutCard.axaml).
       Width 16 fixes the column independently of the glyph: all fifteen
       distinct glyphs the settings page carried when this was measured already measured exactly 16 DIP
       wide (fourteen since part B deleted the 当前版本 card and with it the page's only E946), so this
       changes nothing and stops a future glyph from moving the title. Height is deliberately NOT set — the 16 DIP box is
       Segoe Fluent Icons' own line box at FontSize 16, and pinning a height would clip the glyph if the
       Windows 10 fallback face (Segoe MDL2 Assets) measures taller. An Image has no line box to supply
       one, so that ONE row states Height="16" locally; every glyph row still must not. -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Control).tm-card-icon">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,7,0" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The header text column: title over subtitle, nothing else. Center is what pairs with the icon's
       own Center so both land on one y, the header being always two lines.

       The gutter to the trailing control was 12 and is 11, and the icon's own gutter above was 14 and
       is 7. Both are PUBLISHED by
       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout: an icon and the label it
       marks are related controls (4 DLU / 7 relative pixels), a label and the control it governs are
       further apart than that, and 11 is the guide's "between unrelated controls" step as well as its
       dialog margin. Verification step 5 of the design's Task 7 reads exactly these two gaps off UI
       Automation rects and expects 7 and 11 times the scale. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-text">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,11,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A value line — a path, a size, a version: a subtitle that is one ellipsized line rather than a
       wrapping paragraph. Used WITH tm-card-sub (Classes="tm-card-sub tm-card-value") so it inherits
       size, colour and line height and states only the three things that differ.

       THERE IS NO MaxWidth HERE ANY MORE, AND RE-ADDING ONE NEEDS THIS PARAGRAPH READ FIRST. The class
       shipped with MaxWidth="260" — a MEASURE-time bound, on the argument that a NoWrap +
       CharacterEllipsis TextBlock reports its full untrimmed single-line advance as its desired width
       (trimming is a paint-time concern), so one long path would inflate the card's desired width and
       push the trailing controls past the visible edge. That mechanism was measured when a path line
       lived in column 1, BESIDE those controls. Round 5 moved every value line onto a Grid.Row="1"
       ColumnSpan="2" expansion row, and it does not reach there — re-measured rather than reasoned:
       with the Setter deleted and DataPathText fed a ~250-character path still ellipsized at 1182.00 DIP
       on the widest row this page has — so at least twice the row it sits on below — at the app's own
       floor (then MainWindow's MinWidth="760"; SetWindowPos
       clamped at GetWindowRect 1162 px = 760 DIP client, card 664.00 DIP, content box 629.33, expansion
       span 599.33 — Round 7, Task 8 moved the floor to a 872 DIP WINDOW, which is the SAME 711 DIP
       content pane and therefore the same 664.00 DIP card and the same three figures below it; that is
       exactly why the minimum moved by the pane's own 112 DIP rather than staying at 760) in BOTH
       languages: every card still measures 664.00 DIP with unchanged heights, every
       trailing control still lands on the content right edge (0.00 DIP over; 12.00 on the five toggle
       rows, the documented tm-card-toggle overhang), and SettingsScroll still reports
       HorizontallyScrollable=False with a vertical ScrollBar as its only one. It cannot reach there:
       SettingsPage's ScrollViewer never enables horizontal scrolling, so ScrollContentPresenter measures
       CardStack against the real viewport width, the card Border stretches to it, and a spanning child's
       desired width has nowhere to inflate into.

       What the bound did cost was visible: DataPathText sat clamped at 259.33 DIP and already ellipsized
       (258 DIP of ink) on a row measured at 1185.33 DIP at the app's fitted width — some 925 DIP of
       empty space immediately right of a truncated path. That same real path measures 522.67 DIP
       untrimmed and now renders whole. It still ellipsizes when the row genuinely runs out (591.33 DIP
       of the probe path at the floor), which is what the trimming is for.

       HorizontalAlignment="Left" STAYS, but its job changed with the bound's removal. It is no longer
       what stops the accidental centring — Avalonia 12.1.0's Layoutable.ArrangeCore clamps the arranged
       width to MaxWidth and then runs the Stretch case THROUGH Center's centring arithmetic (the two
       labels share one switch case), so a MaxWidth with no stated alignment centres the line, which is
       exactly what put the two folder rows' path lines 384.67 and 456.67 DIP right of their own titles
       on the shipped build. With no MaxWidth there is nothing to centre. What Left does now is keep the
       element's bounds equal to its TEXT: a Stretch line arranges to the whole span instead — measured
       against UpdateHelpText, a plain tm-card-sub on the same kind of row, which reports 1185.33 DIP
       fitted and 599.33 at the floor, the full span both times — which would spread the two path lines'
       ToolTip across the empty width beside them. The centring mechanism is separately load-bearing in
       SettingsPage.axaml, which uses it deliberately to centre CardStack at its own cap.

       THE ToolTip RULE SURVIVES THE BOUND, restated against the row rather than against 260: a value
       line whose text CAN outgrow the row it sits on and has no ToolTip is a defect in that card, and
       supplying one is the CONSUMER'S job, not this class's. FoldersCard's two path lines are unbounded
       user data — a long one still ellipsizes at the floor — and set a matching ToolTip in code
       (FoldersCard.axaml.cs:102, 138, 220). Its third value line, DataInfoText, sets none on purpose:
       its text is the fixed CurrentDataSize / CurrentDataPending format over one of four short kind
       literals plus a formatted byte count, and it measures 124.67 DIP Chinese / 142.00 English against
       the floor's 599.33 span, so it can never be trimmed and a ToolTip would only repeat the line
       already on screen. AboutCard's two lines (91.33 and 186.00) go without one for the same reason. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-value">
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="NoWrap" />
    <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The expansion row's own top gap, on the child rather than on the Grid — see the RowSpacing note
       above. 7 rather than the 8 it shipped with, and 8 rather than the 10 three of the old below-row
       blocks stated before that: 10 was on no ladder at all, 8 was Fluent's rung, and 7 is
       vis-layout's PUBLISHED "Between related controls — 4 / 7". Part B put every action strip in
       column 2, so what this class now spaces is full-width expansion content — FoldersCard's value
       lines, ToolsCard's bordered list, UpdatesCard's help line and progress bar. -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Control).tm-card-expander">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,7,0,0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-expander">
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="7" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Value lines stack tighter than blocks do. Declared after tm-card-expander so a panel carrying both
       classes resolves to 2: at equal specificity the later rule wins, and a stack OF value lines is
       what the smaller gap is for. The 2 is AUTHORED and is deliberately BELOW the published ladder,
       which starts at 5: vis-layout's rungs separate CONTROLS, and these are consecutive lines inside
       one block of text. Setting it to 5 would open the same gap between two lines of one value that
       the ladder puts between a label and the control it governs. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-values">
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="2" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Every action strip on the page. Part B put them all in column 2, however many buttons they hold:
       the old "three or more go below" rule rested on an overflow at the app's own width floor that was
       re-measured once the value lines left column 1, and no longer exists (see FoldersCard's class
       remark for the numbers, and for why Round 7, Task 8 moved that floor from a 760 DIP window to an
       872 DIP one without moving the 711 DIP content pane it is really about).
       HorizontalAlignment="Right" is therefore inert today — column 2 is Auto-sized to the strip — and
       is kept because it is what would put a full-width expansion-row strip on the content edge, so the
       one class satisfies "every control's right edge is the card's" in either position.
       VerticalAlignment="Center" on the strip is what makes the per-button VerticalAlignment attributes
       unnecessary: a horizontal StackPanel stretches its children to its own height. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-actions">
    <Setter Property="Orientation" Value="Horizontal" />
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="7" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The settings toggle. Blanking OnContent/OffContent moves out of the cards that each repeat it:
       Avalonia's default is the untranslated English "On"/"Off", which would sit next to a Chinese row
       label whenever AppLanguage is zh-Hans.

       THE NEGATIVE RIGHT MARGIN IS NOT SLOP — do not remove it without re-reading this. The template
       behind this class builds an inner Grid of three columns: Auto for the pill, then a hard 12 pixel
       column with MaxWidth 12, then Auto for the two content presenters. That fixed spacer is charged
       whether or not the presenters are empty, so blanking the content collapses column 3 and leaves
       column 2: the element measures 12 DIP wider than its visible pill, and the pill's ink stopped
       12 DIP short of every other row's control on the build before this class existed. A
       ColumnDefinition is not a Control, so no /template/ selector can reach it. The margin makes the
       desired width the pill's own, so the Auto column measures the pill and ArrangeCore deflates the
       slot by the negative margin — the pill lands on the content edge and the 12 DIP of empty presenter
       overhangs into the row's own right padding, where it draws nothing. Round 7, Task 7 cut that
       padding from 16 to 11 (Border.tm-card above), so the empty presenter now overhangs the padding
       by 1 DIP instead of sitting 4 DIP inside it. That changes nothing measurable: the presenters
       are blank, the element's DESIRED width is already the pill's own, and the row's width is set by
       the ScrollViewer's viewport rather than by this column — so what the overhang covers is one DIP
       of a surface nobody draws on.

       Round 7, Task 3 replaced the template underneath and the margin SURVIVED UNCHANGED, because the
       vendored Aero ToggleSwitch copies that inner grid verbatim, `ColumnDefinition Width="12"
       MaxWidth="12"` included (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ToggleSwitch.axaml) — identical to Fluent
       12.1.0's, decompiled. The pill itself did change, and every number below is a fresh UI Automation
       BoundingRectangle read on the live 设置 page at this machine's 150 % scale, on the same four
       toggles, on a build with this task's changes and a build without:

         element bounds   78 x 48 physical  ->  102 x 51   (52 x 32 DIP -> 68 x 34)
         pill             40 x 20 DIP       ->   56 x 22   (VENDORED Width/Height/CornerRadius 10)
         element LEFT     x = 4739 physical ->  x = 4715   (16 DIP further left, as the pill grew)
         element RIGHT    x = 4817 physical ->  x = 4817   (UNCHANGED)

       The right edge is the one that matters and it did not move: 4817 minus the 12 DIP (18 physical)
       of empty presenter is 4799, which is the same screen column as the right edge of every TmButton
       on the page — PinTaskbarBtn, HotkeyClearBtn, PickRootBtn and RestoreBtn all end at 4799 in the
       same capture. If a future Avalonia or a future vendored file changes that column, the toggle rows
       shift by whatever the change is. -->
  <Style Selector="ToggleSwitch.tm-card-toggle">
    <Setter Property="OnContent" Value="" />
    <Setter Property="OffContent" Value="" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,-12,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The hotkey capture box (BehaviorCard's HotkeyBox). It IS legitimately disabled whenever
       启用全局热键 is off (BehaviorCard's UpdateHotkeyEnabledState), and that logic is correct and
       untouched. What this class states now is only its SIZE and its type; its paint is the theme's.

       ── ROUND 7, TASK 7 DELETED THE FIVE PAINT RULES THIS CLASS USED TO CARRY ─────────────────────
       They existed for one reason, stated in full when Round 3 wrote them: the box was the ONE control
       on the 设置 page carrying no Classes and no Theme, so it fell through to the stock Fluent text
       box, whose disabled fill was a heavy grey block standing next to buttons that greyed out gently
       — two disabled vocabularies on one page. Four rules gave it the page's vocabulary instead, and
       Task 4 re-aimed them from Fluent's Border#PART_BorderElement onto Aero's Border#BgElement and
       re-measured the match: box and TmButton read the identical triple in every state, in both
       variants.

       That premise no longer holds, and the rules were the more expensive half of a problem that has
       gone away. The page has ONE vocabulary now because every control on it is Aero's — the box, the
       每列个数 NumericUpDown beside it, the three ComboBoxes and the buttons all resolve to a vendored
       Aero theme — and the two brushes those rules named (SurfaceLayerBrush, SurfaceLayerActiveBrush)
       are deleted. Re-pointing them at Aero's own text-box brushes would have been five rules that
       repaint a part with the value it already has, which is the kind of rule this round's
       dead-selector sweep exists to remove. So the box shows
       Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml's own four states, and the disabled comparison the old
       block guarded is now true by construction rather than by measurement.

       ONE SIGNAL CHANGES HANDS AND IT MATTERS. "Click the box, then press the combination" IS the
       interaction (the tunneling KeyDown handler in BehaviorCard's ctor), so the box has to say when
       it is listening. That used to be an accent hairline this file painted; it is now Aero's own
       AeroTextBoxFocusBorderBrush over AeroTextBoxFocusBackground, which is a real, visible focus
       repaint rather than nothing — the same repaint the search field's own theme rules further up
       this file describe suppressing, for the different reason that the field's fill is drawn by the
       Border around it. The Round 7 Task 7 report carries the capture. -->

  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="160" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
    <!-- Vertical padding 0, exactly as TmButton states it, and for the same reason: the height then
         comes from MinHeight rather than from font metrics plus padding, so the box matches the button
         standing beside it in the same action strip. The horizontal 8 became 7 with the rest of the
         page's spacing (vis-layout's published "between related controls"); the vertical 0 is not a
         spacing value at all and is unchanged. Measured on the live page at 150 % when this was
         written, reading the box's UIA rect at each of the eight scroll stops: with a vertical 5 it
         was 50/50/49/48/49/49 physical px against a TmButton's flat 48 at every stop, and because a
         horizontal StackPanel stretches its children to its own height, 清除 was stretched to the same
         50. With 0 it is 49/48/48/47/48/48 — the same value as the button at every stop but one, and
         the ±1 that remains is rect quantisation, not layout. Setting an explicit Height instead of
         MinHeight was tried and measured byte-for-byte identical, so MinHeight stays — it is what
         TmButton uses and it cannot clip a taller font fallback. VerticalContentAlignment centres the
         text in whatever height MinHeight settles on. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The two headings ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       The five card-group headings (外观 / 行为 / 文件夹 / 常用工具 / 更新) and the page's own title
       (设置). Round 3, Task 3 separated them because they shared one class at one size and the page
       title read as just another subsection; the separation stands, and both sides of it are now
       PUBLISHED rather than derived from a Task Manager capture.

       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-fonts gives Windows 7 exactly two
       text roles above body: a MAIN INSTRUCTION at 12 pt in blue, and GROUP HEADERS at 11 pt in the
       same blue. 12 pt at 96 dpi is 16 DIP (FontSizeSubtitle, and the vendored AeroFontSizeLarge
       agrees exactly); 11 pt is 14.667, rounded up to FontSizeGroupHead's 15. Both take HeadingBrush,
       which is that blue — Tokens.axaml carries the value, because this file may not quote a hex
       colour anywhere, prose included.

       WHAT THE ROUND 3 MEASUREMENT SAID, and why it is recorded rather than cited: live ink-height
       sampling of Task Manager's settings page put its page title's ink at about 1.4x its section
       headings', and the Fluent ramp's 20:14 = 1.43 matched it. Aero's published pair is 16:15 = 1.07,
       so SIZE barely separates them any more and the separation is carried by weight and by one ladder
       step instead. That is Aero's own answer rather than a compromise: a Windows 7 property sheet
       takes its hierarchy from colour first, and it draws main instructions and group headers one
       point apart on purpose.

       The group heading's 0,20,0,8 margin becomes 0,22,0,7. 22 is the stated 2 x Space3 that replaces
       the deleted Space5 (Tokens.axaml), and 7 is vis-layout's "between related controls" — a heading
       and the group it labels are as related as two controls get. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-group-head">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeGroupHead}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource HeadingBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,22,0,7" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-page-title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource HeadingBrush}" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
